(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["llms",{"c:tokens-and-tokenisation":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A model never sees letters or words. A \u003cb>tokeniser\u003c/b> splits text into pieces called \u003cb>tokens\u003c/b> and maps each one to an integer ID from a fixed \u003cb>vocabulary\u003c/b> (typically 100,000 to 250,000 entries). Common English words are usually one token; rare words, other scripts, digits and code split into several. Tokens are the unit of everything you pay for and every limit you hit: price, context window, output length and speed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>From text to IDs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Here is what OpenAI's \u003ccode>o200k_base\u003c/code> tokeniser (used by its recent models) does with a familiar question, measured with the \u003ccode>tiktoken\u003c/code> library:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"How many r's are in strawberry?\"   -&gt;  8 tokens\n['How', ' many', ' r', \"'s\", ' are', ' in', ' strawberry', '?']\n\n\"strawberry\"  (no leading space)    -&gt;  3 tokens: ['st', 'raw', 'berry']\n\" strawberry\" (leading space)       -&gt;  1 token:  [' strawberry']   id 101830\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three things to notice:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spaces belong to the token that follows them.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>' strawberry'\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>'strawberry'\u003c/code> are different tokens with different IDs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Case and position change the split.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>'Strawberry'\u003c/code> becomes \u003ccode>'Str'\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>'aw'\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>'berry'\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The model receives IDs, not letters.\u003c/b> To count the r's in token 101830 it must have learnt that token's spelling indirectly from training data. That is why letter-counting questions trip up models that write good code.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>How byte-pair encoding builds a vocabulary\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Most LLM tokenisers use \u003cb>byte-pair encoding (BPE)\u003c/b>. Start from single bytes (256 symbols, so any text in any language can be encoded), then repeatedly merge the most frequent adjacent pair into a new symbol until the vocabulary reaches its target size. A toy corpus of four words shows the mechanism:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Corpus: low x5, lower x2, newest x6, widest x3      (start: single letters)\n\nMerge 1: e + s   (pair seen 9 times) -&gt; n e w es t,  w i d es t\nMerge 2: es + t  (9)                 -&gt; n e w est,   w i d est\nMerge 3: l + o   (7)                 -&gt; lo w,        lo w e r\nMerge 4: lo + w  (7)                 -&gt; low,         low e r\nMerge 5: n + e   (6)                 -&gt; ne w est\nMerge 6: ne + w  (6)                 -&gt; new est\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After six merges the vocabulary contains \u003ccode>low\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>est\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>new\u003c/code>. A word it never saw, \"lowest\", now tokenises as \u003ccode>low\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>est\u003c/code>: two tokens and no unknown symbol. Real tokenisers learn 100,000 to 200,000 merges from huge amounts of text, after a pre-tokenisation step that splits on spaces, punctuation and digit groups. The merges are then frozen; at run time the tokeniser just applies them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why some text costs more\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The merges reflect the training text, which is mostly English web pages and code. Text that is rare in that mix gets fewer merges and therefore more tokens. Counts for the same sentences under three generations of OpenAI tokenisers:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Text\u003c/th>\u003cth>Characters\u003c/th>\u003cth>GPT-2 (50k vocab)\u003c/th>\u003cth>cl100k_base (100k)\u003c/th>\u003cth>o200k_base (200k)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>English sentence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>102\u003c/td>\u003ctd>21\u003c/td>\u003ctd>21\u003c/td>\u003ctd>21\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Python snippet (5 lines)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>78\u003c/td>\u003ctd>39\u003c/td>\u003ctd>29\u003c/td>\u003ctd>29\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>German sentence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>68\u003c/td>\u003ctd>26\u003c/td>\u003ctd>19\u003c/td>\u003ctd>15\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hindi sentence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>74\u003c/td>\u003ctd>120\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80\u003c/td>\u003ctd>26\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Japanese sentence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>29\u003c/td>\u003ctd>34\u003c/td>\u003ctd>28\u003c/td>\u003ctd>21\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The number 1234567.891\u003c/td>\u003ctd>11\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>English: The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. Tokenisation splits text into pieces the model can count.\nGerman:  Die Tokenisierung zerlegt Text in Teile, die das Modell zählen kann.\nHindi:   टोकनाइज़ेशन टेक्स्ट को ऐसे टुकड़ों में बाँटता है जिन्हें मॉडल गिन सकता है।\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>What the table teaches:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>English is cheap everywhere\u003c/b>: about 4 to 5 characters per token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Other languages depend heavily on the tokeniser.\u003c/b> The same Hindi sentence costs 120 tokens with GPT-2's vocabulary and 26 with o200k_base. Even with a modern vocabulary it uses about twice as many tokens per word as English, so it is slower, pricier and fills the context faster.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Code spends tokens on whitespace and symbols.\u003c/b> GPT-2 used one token per indentation space; newer vocabularies have tokens for runs of spaces, which is why the snippet fell from 39 to 29.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Numbers are chunked\u003c/b>, here into groups of up to three digits (\u003ccode>123\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>456\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>7\u003c/code>), so the model never sees place value directly. That is one reason exact arithmetic is unreliable without a tool.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Rules of thumb\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>English prose: one token is about 4 characters or three quarters of a word. 1,000 words is about 1,300 tokens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Code, JSON and non-Latin scripts: expect 1.5 to 3 times more tokens per word than English prose, and measure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Model families use different tokenisers (vocabulary sizes: Llama 3 has 128,256 tokens, Qwen3 151,936, gpt-oss 201,088), so the same prompt has a different token count at each provider. Compare cost per task, not price per token.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count with the model's own tokeniser: \u003ccode>tiktoken\u003c/code> for OpenAI models, \u003ccode>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(...)\u003c/code> from Hugging Face for open models, or the provider's token-counting endpoint (Anthropic offers one). Never estimate from words when you are near a limit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Limits such as \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> are in tokens, so \"answer in 200 words\" and \u003ccode>max_tokens=200\u003c/code> are different constraints.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Special tokens\u003c/b> (end of turn, tool-call markers) are single IDs that normal text never produces. You meet them in the chat templates lesson.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">If you tokenise the same short message in English, as Python code and in another language, how do the token counts compare, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>English is the cheapest, about 4 to 5 characters per token (21 tokens for a 102-character sentence with o200k_base). Code costs more per character because whitespace, brackets and operators take tokens (a 78-character snippet was 29 tokens). Other languages cost more, by an amount that depends on the tokeniser: a Hindi sentence was 120 tokens with GPT-2's vocabulary, 80 with cl100k_base and 26 with o200k_base. The cause is BPE: merges are learnt mostly from English text and code, so rarer scripts get fewer long tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a model write a poem about strawberries but miscount the r's in \"strawberry\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It receives the word as one or a few token IDs (\u003ccode>' strawberry'\u003c/code> is a single token), not as letters. Counting letters requires recalling the spelling of that ID, which it only learnt indirectly. Asking it to spell the word letter by letter first, or to use code, fixes it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Roughly how many tokens is a 3,000-word English report, and how would you get the exact number?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 4,000 tokens (3,000 divided by 0.75). For the exact number, run the text through the target model's tokeniser or the provider's token-counting endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You move a workload to a provider with the same price per million tokens, but your bill rises by 15%. What is a likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The new model's tokeniser splits your text into more tokens, for example because your content is code or a language its vocabulary covers less well.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Try it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import tiktoken  # pip install tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"o200k_base\")\nfor text in [\"Hello world\", \"def add(a, b):\\n    return a + b\", \"Die Tokenisierung zerlegt Text.\"]:\n    ids = enc.encode(text)\n    print(len(ids), [enc.decode([i]) for i in ids])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Add a sentence in a language you know and a long number. Predict the counts before you run it.\u003c/p>","l:llms/embeddings-meaning-as-vectors":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The first layer of an LLM is a lookup table, the \u003cb>embedding matrix\u003c/b>: one row of numbers per token ID. Training adjusts the rows so tokens used in similar ways get similar vectors, which turns meaning into geometry. Inside the model each token's vector becomes a \u003cb>residual stream\u003c/b>: every layer reads it and adds an update, and by the last layer it encodes the model's prediction for the next token.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The embedding table\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If the vocabulary has V tokens and the model width (its \u003cb>hidden size\u003c/b>) is d, the embedding matrix has V rows and d columns. Looking up a token is just selecting a row:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import numpy as np\n\nV, d = 1_000, 64                  # real Llama 3.1 8B: V = 128,256 and d = 4,096\nE = np.random.randn(V, d) * 0.02  # learnt during training; random here\ntoken_ids = [791, 42, 517]        # three token IDs from the tokeniser\nx = E[token_ids]                  # shape (3, 64): one vector per token\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The real table is big. In Llama 3.1 8B it is 128,256 × 4,096 = 525 million numbers, about 1.05 GB in 16-bit precision and roughly 6.5% of the whole model. A second matrix of the same shape sits at the top, the \u003cb>unembedding\u003c/b> or \u003cb>LM head\u003c/b>, which turns the final vector back into one score per vocabulary token. Some small models reuse (\"tie\") the same matrix for both to save parameters.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why directions carry meaning\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Nobody writes these numbers by hand. During pretraining, gradient descent nudges each row so the model predicts the next token better. Tokens that appear in similar contexts (\"cat\" and \"dog\" both appear near \"food\", \"vet\" and \"pet\") receive similar updates and drift together. This is the distributional idea: you know a word by the company it keeps.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Closeness is measured with \u003cb>cosine similarity\u003c/b>, the cosine of the angle between two vectors: 1 means the same direction, 0 means unrelated.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>cos(a, b) = (a · b) / (|a| × |b|)\n\nToy vectors:  cat = [0.8, 0.1, 0.6]   dog = [0.7, 0.2, 0.7]   car = [0.1, 0.9, 0.2]\n\ncos(cat, dog) = 1.00 / (1.005 × 1.010) = 0.985\ncos(cat, car) = 0.29 / (1.005 × 0.927) = 0.311\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Directions can also encode relationships. The famous word2vec result from 2013 is that vector(\"king\") − vector(\"man\") + vector(\"woman\") lands nearest to vector(\"queen\"): a gender direction and a royalty direction are roughly separable. In an LLM with thousands of dimensions, many features (topic, tense, language, sentiment, \"this is code\") are stored as directions at the same time, overlapping each other. Treat the arithmetic as intuition, not an exact law.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Static at the bottom, contextual above\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The embedding table gives \"bank\" the same vector in \"river bank\" and \"bank account\". Context enters only through attention in the layers above. After a few layers the two \"bank\" vectors differ, because each has absorbed information from its neighbours. When people talk about a model's representation of a token, they almost always mean these contextual vectors, not the raw table row.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The residual stream\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Layers do not replace a token's vector; they add to it:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>x0 = embedding row for the token\nx1 = x0 + attention_update(x0)      # mix in information from earlier tokens\nx2 = x1 + mlp_update(x1)            # process this token on its own\n...  repeated for every layer  ...\nlogits = final_vector × unembedding  # one score per vocabulary token\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This running sum is the \u003cb>residual stream\u003c/b>. It keeps the same width (4,096 in Llama 3.1 8B) from bottom to top, like a shared whiteboard that each layer reads and writes. Two consequences: information written early can survive to the top unchanged, which helps very deep models train stably; and interpretability researchers study models by finding directions in this stream that correspond to concepts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>LLM token embeddings are not search embeddings.\u003c/b> For semantic search you use a dedicated \u003cb>embedding model\u003c/b> that turns a whole text into one vector trained for similarity (Embeddings module). Do not average rows of an LLM's table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Vocabulary size is a trade-off.\u003c/b> A bigger vocabulary means fewer tokens per text (cheaper, more fits in context) but a larger embedding table and output layer, and a slower softmax over the vocabulary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Width multiplies memory.\u003c/b> Every token in flight carries d-sized vectors through every layer, which is part of why the KV cache (next module) is so large.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How many parameters are in the embedding table of a model with a 151,936-token vocabulary and hidden size 4,096, and how much memory is that in 16-bit precision?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>151,936 × 4,096 = about 622 million parameters; at 2 bytes each, about 1.24 GB. An untied output layer of the same shape doubles that.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \"bank\" have the same vector in two sentences at the input but different vectors at layer 20?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The input embedding is a fixed lookup by token ID. Attention layers then add information from surrounding tokens into each token's residual stream, so by layer 20 each \"bank\" carries its context (river or money).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the residual stream?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The per-token vector that runs through the whole model. Each attention and MLP layer reads it and adds its output to it instead of replacing it; the final value is projected onto the vocabulary to produce next-token scores.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should you not use an LLM's input embedding table for semantic search?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its rows describe single tokens without context and were trained for next-token prediction, not for comparing whole texts. Embedding models are trained specifically so that texts with similar meaning get nearby vectors.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:self-attention":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Attention lets each token gather information from earlier tokens. Every token produces a \u003cb>query\u003c/b> (what am I looking for?), a \u003cb>key\u003c/b> (what do I offer?) and a \u003cb>value\u003c/b> (what I pass on if chosen). A token's query is compared with every key, softmax turns the scores into weights, and the output is the weighted average of the values. A \u003cb>causal mask\u003c/b> stops tokens seeing the future, and many \u003cb>heads\u003c/b> run in parallel to track different relationships.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The recipe\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each token vector x (one row of the residual stream), three learnt matrices produce three smaller vectors:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>q = x · Wq      k = x · Wk      v = x · Wv        (each of length d_head, e.g. 128)\n\nFor the current token's query q against every visible token j:\nscore_j  = (q · k_j) / sqrt(d_head)\nweight_j = softmax(scores)_j         # all positive, sum to 1\noutput   = sum over j of weight_j × v_j\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In matrix form for all tokens at once: \u003ccode>Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q·Kᵀ / sqrt(d_head) + mask) · V\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An analogy: queries are search terms, keys are the labels on folders and values are the folders' contents. The query is compared with every label and you get back a blend of contents, weighted by how well each label matched.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why divide by the square root of d_head? The dot product of two random 128-number vectors grows with their length. Unscaled, the scores get so large that softmax puts nearly all the weight on one token and training stalls. Scaling keeps softmax soft.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A worked example by hand\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Three tokens, \"The cat sat\", with d_head = 2. Suppose the learnt projections have produced these keys and values:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Token\u003c/th>\u003cth>Key k\u003c/th>\u003cth>Value v\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[1, 0]\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[1, 0]\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>cat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[0, 1]\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[0, 2]\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>sat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[1, 1]\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[1, 1]\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Compute the attention output for \"sat\", whose query is q = [0, 2]:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>raw scores q·k:   The = 0      cat = 2      sat = 2\nscaled (÷ 1.414):       0            1.414        1.414\nexp:                    1.000        4.113        4.113     (sum 9.226)\nweights:                0.108        0.446        0.446\noutput = 0.108 × [1, 0] + 0.446 × [0, 2] + 0.446 × [1, 1]  ≈  [0.55, 1.34]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\"sat\" attends mostly to \"cat\" and to itself, so its new vector is built mostly from their values. Training shapes Wq and Wk so that useful pairs (a verb and its subject, a closing bracket and its opener, a name and its earlier mention) produce high scores.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The causal mask\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When \"cat\" is processed it may look at \"The\" and at itself, but not at \"sat\", which comes later. Decoder LLMs enforce this by adding minus infinity to the scores of future positions before softmax, which makes their weights exactly zero:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>              keys:  The     cat     sat\nquery The         [  ok      -inf    -inf ]\nquery cat         [  ok      ok      -inf ]\nquery sat         [  ok      ok      ok   ]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The mask makes training efficient (every position learns to predict its next token in the same pass) and has a consequence the next module builds on: a token's keys and values never change when later tokens arrive, so they can be computed once and cached.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Multi-head attention\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One pattern is not enough: a token may need its subject, the previous token and a matching quote mark all at once. So the model runs h \u003cb>heads\u003c/b> in parallel, each with its own Wq, Wk and Wv and a head size of d/h. Llama 3.1 8B has 32 heads of 128 dimensions (32 × 128 = 4,096). The heads' outputs are concatenated, mixed by an output matrix Wo and added to the residual stream. Researchers have found heads that track the previous token, heads that copy repeated patterns (\"induction heads\") and heads that follow syntax.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import numpy as np\n\ndef causal_self_attention(X, Wq, Wk, Wv):\n    Q, K, V = X @ Wq, X @ Wk, X @ Wv                    # (n, d_head) each\n    scores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(K.shape[1])             # (n, n)\n    scores[np.triu_indices(len(X), k=1)] = -np.inf     # hide future tokens\n    w = np.exp(scores - scores.max(axis=1, keepdims=True))\n    w /= w.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)                  # softmax per row\n    return w @ V                                        # (n, d_head)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why this matters to you\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost grows with the square of length.\u003c/b> The score matrix is n × n per head per layer. At 128,000 tokens that is about 16 billion scores per head. Optimised kernels such as FlashAttention avoid storing the matrix, but the compute remains, which is why long prompts take longer to process.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Attention is a budget.\u003c/b> Weights sum to 1, so every extra token competes for a share. Long, noisy contexts dilute attention on what matters: one intuition behind \"lost in the middle\" and context rot (Context module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generation reuses keys and values.\u003c/b> A new token needs only its own query against all stored keys. That store is the KV cache, the centre of the next module.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain what queries, keys and values are in one paragraph.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each token's vector is projected by three learnt matrices into a query, a key and a value. The query describes what the token is looking for, the key describes what a token offers to be found by, and the value is the information it hands over. Attention scores the current token's query against every visible key with a scaled dot product, turns the scores into weights with softmax, and returns the weighted sum of the values, so the token's new representation is a blend of the tokens most relevant to it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why are attention scores divided by the square root of d_head?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Dot products grow with vector length, so unscaled scores would be large and softmax would become almost one-hot, giving tiny gradients and unstable training. Scaling keeps the weights soft.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the worked example, why does \"sat\" take almost nothing from \"The\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its query [0, 2] has a dot product of 0 with the key of \"The\" ([1, 0]) against 2 for \"cat\" and \"sat\", so after scaling and softmax \"The\" gets a weight of 0.11 and the other two 0.45 each.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does the causal mask do, and why does it make caching possible?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It sets the scores of future positions to minus infinity, so each token attends only to itself and earlier tokens. Since no token is influenced by later ones, its keys and values are fixed once computed and can be stored and reused during generation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:embeddings-and-the-transformer":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A decoder-only LLM is three things: an embedding table, a stack of identical \u003cb>blocks\u003c/b> (attention then an MLP, each wrapped in normalisation and a residual add), and an output layer that scores every token in the vocabulary. Attention moves information between tokens; the MLP processes each token on its own and holds much of the model's knowledge. Generation repeats one forward pass per new token.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Inside one block\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Current open models (Llama, Qwen, Mistral, DeepSeek, gpt-oss) use the \"pre-norm\" layout:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>h   = x + Attention(RMSNorm(x))     # read from other tokens, add to the stream\nout = h + MLP(RMSNorm(h))           # transform this token, add to the stream\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>RMSNorm\u003c/b> rescales each vector to a standard size (then multiplies by learnt gains) so numbers stay in a healthy range through dozens of layers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Attention\u003c/b> is the multi-head mechanism from the previous lesson, with positions added by RoPE (next lesson).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The MLP\u003c/b> (also called the feed-forward network) is applied to each token independently. Most current models use a \u003cb>SwiGLU\u003c/b> MLP: two projections up to a wider size, one passed through a smooth gate function and multiplied by the other, then one projection back down. In Llama 3.1 8B the widths go 4,096 to 14,336 and back to 4,096.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Residual adds\u003c/b> (the \u003ccode>x +\u003c/code>) keep information flowing even when a block contributes little.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Stack 32 of these (Llama 3.1 8B), 36 (Qwen3-8B) or 80 (Llama 3.1 70B), add a final RMSNorm and the LM head, and you have the model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where the parameters are\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All the numbers come from the model's \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code> on Hugging Face. For Llama 3.1 8B (hidden size 4,096, 32 layers, 32 query heads, 8 key-value heads of 128 dimensions, MLP width 14,336, vocabulary 128,256):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Part\u003c/th>\u003cth>Calculation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Parameters\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Embedding table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128,256 × 4,096\u003c/td>\u003ctd>525 M\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Attention, per layer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wq and Wo 4,096 × 4,096 each; Wk and Wv 4,096 × 1,024 each\u003c/td>\u003ctd>41.9 M\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MLP, per layer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 matrices of 4,096 × 14,336\u003c/td>\u003ctd>176.2 M\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>All 32 layers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32 × (41.9 M + 176.2 M + norms)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6,980 M\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LM head\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,096 × 128,256\u003c/td>\u003ctd>525 M\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Total\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>8,030 M\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>About 70% of the parameters sit in the MLPs. The key and value projections are narrower than the query projection (1,024 against 4,096) because this model uses grouped-query attention, covered later in this module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Trace one token\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Follow the last token of \"The capital of France is\" through the model:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Token ID\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Embedding row\u003c/b>\u003csmall>4,096 numbers\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>32 blocks\u003c/b>\u003csmall>attention + MLP add to the stream\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Final RMSNorm\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>LM head\u003c/b>\u003csmall>128,256 logits\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Softmax\u003c/b>\u003csmall>probabilities\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Sampler\u003c/b>\u003csmall>next token\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>The tokeniser produces IDs; the last one is \u003ccode>' is'\u003c/code>. Its embedding row enters the residual stream.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In each block, attention lets \u003ccode>' is'\u003c/code> read from \u003ccode>' France'\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>' capital'\u003c/code>; the MLP then transforms the result, writing associated facts into the stream.\u003c/li>\u003cli>After the last block the vector is normalised and multiplied by the LM head, giving one \u003cb>logit\u003c/b> (an unnormalised score) per vocabulary token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Softmax\u003c/b> turns logits into probabilities: p_i = exp(z_i) divided by the sum of exp(z_j) over all tokens. If the top logits were \u003ccode>' Paris'\u003c/code> 9.1, \u003ccode>' a'\u003c/code> 6.3 and \u003ccode>' the'\u003c/code> 5.0 (ignoring the rest), the probabilities would be 0.93, 0.06 and 0.02.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A sampler picks a token (Inference module); it is appended and the process runs again for the next position.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Every position produces logits. At generation time only the last position's logits are used; during training every position's prediction is scored against the true next token, so one document teaches thousands of predictions in a single pass.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why decoder-only won\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The 2017 transformer had an \u003cb>encoder\u003c/b> (reads the whole input with attention in both directions) and a \u003cb>decoder\u003c/b> (generates output with causal attention), designed for translation. GPT models kept only the decoder: questions, documents and conversations all become one sequence to continue. That single objective scales simply and covers every task, so nearly every chat and reasoning model in 2026 is decoder-only. Encoder-only models (the BERT family) live on in embedding models, rerankers and classifiers, where reading in both directions helps.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice: read a config.json\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Before you pick, serve or fine-tune an open model, open its \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code>. The fields that matter:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003cth>Llama 3.1 8B\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>hidden_size\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Width d of the residual stream\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4096\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>num_hidden_layers\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Number of blocks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>num_attention_heads\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Query heads\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>num_key_value_heads\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Key-value heads; fewer than query heads means GQA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>head_dim\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Size per head; if absent, hidden_size ÷ query heads\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>intermediate_size\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>MLP inner width\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14336\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>vocab_size\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vocabulary size\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128256\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_position_embeddings\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Longest supported context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>131072\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>torch_dtype\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Precision of the released weights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>bfloat16\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With these you can estimate weight memory (parameters × bytes per parameter) and KV-cache memory (Inference module) before downloading anything.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Trace one token through embedding, attention and MLP layers to the next-token prediction.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The token ID selects a row of the embedding table, a vector of hidden-size numbers that starts the residual stream. In each block, RMSNorm then attention lets the token read from earlier tokens and adds the result to the stream; RMSNorm then the MLP transforms the token on its own and adds that too. After the last block a final norm is applied and the LM head turns the vector into one logit per vocabulary token; softmax converts logits to probabilities and a sampler picks the next token, which is appended for the next pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which part of a block moves information between token positions, and which works on each position separately?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Attention moves information between positions. The MLP and the norms operate on each position independently.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Roughly what share of Llama 3.1 8B's parameters are in its MLPs, and how do you know?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 70%. Each layer's MLP has 3 × 4,096 × 14,336 = 176 million parameters against 42 million for attention, and 32 layers of MLPs total about 5.6 billion of the model's 8.03 billion parameters.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does the LM head output, and how large is it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One logit per vocabulary token for each position. It is a hidden-size by vocabulary-size matrix: 4,096 × 128,256 = 525 million parameters in Llama 3.1 8B.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:llms/positions-rope-and-extending-context":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Attention on its own ignores order: shuffle the tokens and each one gets the same weighted blend. Modern LLMs add order with \u003cb>rotary position embeddings (RoPE)\u003c/b>: each query and key is rotated by an angle proportional to its position, so the score between two tokens depends on their \u003ci>distance\u003c/i>. Models are trained at a shorter length and then stretched to long contexts by rescaling those rotations (position interpolation, YaRN) plus a short phase of long-context training.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why order is needed\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Attention computes a weighted sum over values, and a sum does not care about order. Without position information, \"dog bites man\" and \"man bites dog\" would look the same to every layer. The original transformer added fixed sine-wave vectors to the embeddings; GPT-2 learnt one vector per position (only 1,024 of them, so it could go no further). Almost every current open model uses RoPE instead.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Rotary embeddings in one picture\u003c/h4>\u003cp>RoPE splits each query and key (say 128 numbers) into 64 pairs and treats each pair as a point on a plane. At position m, pair i is rotated by the angle m × θ_i. The useful property: the dot product of a rotated query at position m and a rotated key at position n depends only on m − n.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>2D example, theta = 30 degrees per position\nQuery q = [1, 0] at position 3  -&gt; rotated 90 degrees\nKey   k = [1, 0] at position 1  -&gt; rotated 30 degrees\nAngle between them: 60 degrees, so score = cos 60 = 0.5\n\nMove both 10 positions later (13 and 11): rotated 390 and 330 degrees\nAngle between them: still 60 degrees, score still 0.5\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>So attention sees relative distance, which is what language needs (\"the noun two tokens back\"), and nothing is added to the residual stream.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The pairs rotate at different speeds: θ_i = base^(−2i/d). Early pairs spin fast and resolve fine local order; late pairs spin slowly and can separate tokens thousands of positions apart. The \u003cb>base\u003c/b> is \u003ccode>rope_theta\u003c/code> in config.json: 10,000 in the original paper, 500,000 in Llama 3.1 and 1,000,000 in Qwen3. A larger base slows every rotation, leaving room for longer contexts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Stretching the context\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A model trained on 8,000-token sequences has never seen the rotation angles of position 100,000; feed it those and quality collapses. Three families of fixes:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Position interpolation\u003c/b>: divide positions by a factor s, so 32,000 real positions map into the 8,000-position range the model knows. Simple, but it blurs local order and needs some fine-tuning.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Frequency-aware scaling\u003c/b> (NTK-aware scaling, YaRN, Llama 3's scheme): leave the fast-spinning pairs alone, because local order should not change, and interpolate only the slow ones. YaRN also adjusts the attention temperature; its paper reports extending context with about 10 times fewer tokens and 2.5 times fewer training steps than earlier methods.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long-context training\u003c/b>: a final phase on long documents at the new length (Training module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Real configs show the recipe (values from each model's config.json and model card):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>Trained length\u003c/th>\u003cth>Scaling\u003c/th>\u003cth>Factor\u003c/th>\u003cth>Supported length\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Llama 3.1 8B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8,192\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Llama 3 frequency scaling\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>131,072\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DeepSeek-V3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,096\u003c/td>\u003ctd>YaRN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>40\u003c/td>\u003ctd>163,840\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>gpt-oss-120b\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,096\u003c/td>\u003ctd>YaRN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32\u003c/td>\u003ctd>131,072\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Qwen3-8B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32,768\u003c/td>\u003ctd>YaRN, off by default\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>131,072\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Supported is not the same as good.\u003c/b> The RULER benchmark tested 17 long-context models that all claimed 32,000 tokens or more; only half kept satisfactory performance at 32,000. Test your own task at your real lengths (Inference module, context windows).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Static scaling can hurt short inputs.\u003c/b> Qwen's model card notes that serving frameworks apply YaRN with a fixed factor whatever the input length, which may reduce quality on short texts, so it recommends enabling YaRN only when you need long contexts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A bigger number in a setting is not a bigger ability.\u003c/b> Raising the maximum length in vLLM or llama.cpp beyond what the model was trained and scaled for only admits positions it handles badly.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does attention need position information at all?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Attention is a weighted sum over tokens, and a sum gives the same result in any order, so without positions the model could not tell \"dog bites man\" from \"man bites dog\".\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What property makes RoPE a relative position method?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Queries and keys are rotated by angles proportional to their positions, so the dot product of a query at position m and a key at position n depends only on the difference m − n.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A config shows \u003ccode>rope_scaling\u003c/code> with factor 8 and \u003ccode>original_max_position_embeddings\u003c/code> 8192. What does that tell you?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model was pretrained on 8,192-token sequences and its rotations are rescaled by a factor of 8 to support about 131,072 tokens, usually backed by a long-context training phase. Expect weaker quality near the extended limit than at short lengths, and test it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why might you leave YaRN switched off for a chatbot whose prompts are under 8,000 tokens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Frameworks apply the scaling factor to every request, which changes the rotations even for short inputs and can slightly lower quality, while giving no benefit at those lengths.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:next-token-prediction-and-pretraining":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pretraining teaches a model one skill: predict the next token of a huge pile of text. A \u003cb>base model\u003c/b> therefore continues whatever you give it; ask it a question and it may write more questions. \u003cb>Instruction tuning\u003c/b> trains it on conversations wrapped in special tokens, a \u003cb>chat template\u003c/b>, so it learns to answer in the assistant's turn and then stop. Always use exactly the template the model was trained with.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Next-token prediction\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Training shows the model text and, at every position, asks for a probability distribution over the next token. The loss is \u003cb>cross-entropy\u003c/b>: minus the natural log of the probability it gave to the token that actually came next.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Context: \"The capital of France is\"     true next token: \" Paris\"\np(\" Paris\") = 0.90  -&gt;  loss = -ln 0.90 = 0.11\np(\" Paris\") = 0.01  -&gt;  loss = -ln 0.01 = 4.61\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Averaged over trillions of tokens, minimising this loss forces the model to learn spelling, grammar, facts, styles, code and patterns of reasoning, because all of them help predict text. Nothing in the objective says \"be helpful\" or \"answer the question\".\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What a base model does with a question\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Give a base model \u003ccode>What is the capital of France?\u003c/code> and a typical continuation looks like a page it has seen before:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>What is the capital of France?\nWhat is the capital of Germany?\nWhat is the largest city in Spain?\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>It is completing a quiz list, not answering. You can still steer a base model by making the answer the natural continuation of a document, for example with a few \u003ccode>Q: ... A: ...\u003c/code> pairs ending in an open \u003ccode>A:\u003c/code>. Base models are what you fine-tune from, what researchers study, and occasionally the right tool for pure continuation such as code autocomplete.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Chat templates\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Chat models are trained on conversations serialised into one token sequence with special role markers. Two real formats for the same messages:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Qwen3 (ChatML style):\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;system\nYou are a helpful assistant.&lt;|im_end|&gt;\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;user\nWhat is the capital of France?&lt;|im_end|&gt;\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;assistant\n\nLlama 3:\n&lt;|begin_of_text|&gt;&lt;|start_header_id|&gt;system&lt;|end_header_id|&gt;\n\nYou are a helpful assistant.&lt;|eot_id|&gt;&lt;|start_header_id|&gt;user&lt;|end_header_id|&gt;\n\nWhat is the capital of France?&lt;|eot_id|&gt;&lt;|start_header_id|&gt;assistant&lt;|end_header_id|&gt;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>What to notice:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Markers such as \u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>&lt;|eot_id|&gt;\u003c/code> are \u003cb>special tokens\u003c/b>: single IDs reserved for structure (151644 is \u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;\u003c/code> in Qwen3). Hugging Face tokenisers will also turn the literal string \u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;\u003c/code> typed inside user text into that ID unless told otherwise, so servers must escape or split special-token text in user input or users can forge role boundaries (Security module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The template ends with an open assistant header. This \u003cb>generation prompt\u003c/b> makes a reply the most likely continuation; without it the model may carry on with the user's message.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generation stops when the model emits its end-of-turn token (\u003ccode>&lt;|im_end|&gt;\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>&lt;|eot_id|&gt;\u003c/code>), which the server treats as a stop condition.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tools are rendered into the template too. Qwen3's template writes every tool's JSON schema inside \u003ccode>&lt;tools&gt;\u003c/code> tags in the system turn, which is why tool definitions cost input tokens on every call.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>In code, let the tokeniser apply the template that ships with the model:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\")\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a helpful assistant.\"},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is the capital of France?\"}]\nprint(tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>When you call a hosted API you send JSON messages and the provider applies the template. When you self-host or fine-tune, the template is your responsibility.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why template mismatch breaks things\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A model is only reliable on token patterns it was trained on. Common failures:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth>Likely cause\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The model answers, then writes a fake \"user\" turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>End-of-turn token missing from training data, or not configured as a stop token\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>System prompt ignored\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Template has no system slot, or system text placed where the model never saw it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fine-tuned model worse than the original\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Training data formatted with a different template from the one used at inference\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two begin-of-text tokens at the start\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The template adds one and the code adds another\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reply continues the user's sentence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Generation prompt (\u003ccode>add_generation_prompt\u003c/code>) missing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>OpenAI's gpt-oss model card is blunt: the models were trained on the \"harmony\" response format and should only be used with it, as they will not work correctly otherwise.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>apply_chat_template\u003c/code> or the serving engine's chat endpoint; never hand-assemble role strings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When fine-tuning, format training data with the same template you will serve with, and usually compute the loss only on assistant tokens (Fine-tuning module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>After any template change, run your eval suite. Formatting bugs often look like \"the model got dumber\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain why a base model continues text rather than answering.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pretraining only teaches it to predict the next token of documents, so it produces whatever is most likely to follow, such as more quiz questions after a question. Nothing in that objective rewards answering. Answering in an assistant turn and then stopping are learnt later, in instruction tuning on conversations formatted with a chat template.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>add_generation_prompt=True\u003c/code> do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It appends the opening of an assistant turn (for example \u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;assistant\u003c/code> and a newline) so the most likely continuation is the assistant's reply rather than more of the user's message.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your fine-tuned model answers correctly but then generates \u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;user\u003c/code> and an invented next question. What is the likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is not stopping at the end-of-turn token: either the training data lacked \u003ccode>&lt;|im_end|&gt;\u003c/code> after assistant turns, so the model never learnt to emit it, or the server's stop tokens do not include it. Fix the training format and the generation config.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do tool definitions cost input tokens on every request?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The chat template renders each tool's schema as text inside the prompt (Qwen3 puts them in \u003ccode>&lt;tools&gt;\u003c/code> tags in the system turn), so they are tokenised and processed like any other input on every call.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:llms/attention-variants-and-the-kv-cache":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>To generate each new token, a model needs the keys and values of every earlier token in every layer, so servers keep them in memory: the \u003cb>KV cache\u003c/b> (the Inference module covers it in depth). Its size often limits context length and the number of users per GPU more than the weights do. Most changes to attention since 2019 are ways to store less per token: share key-value heads (MQA, GQA), compress them (MLA), keep only recent tokens (sliding window), or replace most attention layers with a fixed-size state (linear and hybrid attention).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The number to watch: KV bytes per token\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>KV bytes per token = 2 (K and V) × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes per value\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every variant below changes one factor in that product. Figures are computed from each model's config.json at 16-bit precision (2 bytes per value).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Variant\u003c/th>\u003cth>Stored per token, per layer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example (from config.json)\u003c/th>\u003cth>KV per token\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-head (MHA)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>K and V for every head\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Llama 2 7B: 32 layers, 32 KV heads of 128\u003c/td>\u003ctd>512 KiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Grouped-query (GQA)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>K and V for a few shared groups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Llama 3.1 8B: 32 layers, 8 KV heads of 128\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128 KiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-query (MQA)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One K and V head shared by all\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Falcon-7B: 32 layers, 1 KV head of 64\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8 KiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-head latent (MLA)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One compressed vector of 512 plus a 64-number position key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DeepSeek-V3: 61 layers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>69 KiB (4.8 MiB if stored as plain MHA)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>K and V for the last W tokens only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mistral 7B v0.1: W = 4,096\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128 KiB, capped at 4,096 tokens\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Alternating window and full\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full layers grow; window layers stay capped\u003c/td>\u003ctd>gpt-oss-120b: 18 full and 18 window-128 layers, 8 KV heads of 64\u003c/td>\u003ctd>36 KiB growing, plus 4.5 MiB fixed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hybrid linear and full\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only 1 layer in 4 keeps a KV cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Qwen3.8 (2.4T): 23 full and 69 linear layers, 4 KV heads of 256\u003c/td>\u003ctd>92 KiB growing, plus a fixed state\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For the same 32,000 tokens of context, the first two rows mean 16 GiB per conversation for the MHA model and 4 GiB for the GQA one, at about the same parameter count.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Sharing heads: MQA and GQA\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In MHA every query head has its own key and value head. \u003cb>Multi-query attention\u003c/b> (Shazeer, 2019) shares one key-value head across all query heads: the cache shrinks by the number of heads and decoding speeds up, because less data is read per step, at some cost in quality. \u003cb>Grouped-query attention\u003c/b> (2023) is the middle ground: query heads are split into groups and each group shares a KV head.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>MHA:              Q1 Q2 Q3 Q4 Q5 Q6 Q7 Q8\n                  K1 K2 K3 K4 K5 K6 K7 K8      (8 KV heads stored)\n\nGQA, 2 groups:    Q1 Q2 Q3 Q4 | Q5 Q6 Q7 Q8\n                      K1      |     K2         (2 KV heads stored)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Llama 3.1 8B has 32 query heads and 8 KV heads, so each KV head serves 4 query heads and the cache is 4 times smaller than MHA. The GQA paper found quality close to MHA with speed close to MQA, and GQA has been the default for most open models since 2023.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Compressing: multi-head latent attention\u003c/h4>\u003cp>DeepSeek's \u003cb>MLA\u003c/b> (DeepSeek-V2, 2024) stores one small latent vector per token per layer (512 numbers in DeepSeek-V3) instead of separate keys and values for its 128 heads. At attention time each head's keys and values are reconstructed from the latent by learnt up-projection matrices, and with some algebra those can be folded into the query and output projections, so the cache never needs expanding. Because RoPE does not survive that folding, MLA keeps a separate 64-number rotary key per token. DeepSeek reported that, compared with its earlier 67B dense model, DeepSeek-V2 cut the KV cache by 93.3% and raised maximum generation throughput 5.76 times.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Looking less far: sliding windows and sparse attention\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>sliding-window\u003c/b> layer attends only to the last W tokens, so its cache stops growing at W and can be a ring buffer. Information from further back still flows upward through stacked layers, but exact recall of distant details suffers, so most models interleave window layers with full layers: Gemma 3 uses five window layers (W = 1,024) per full layer, and gpt-oss alternates them one to one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Sparse attention\u003c/b> keeps every token but lets each query attend to a selected subset. DeepSeek-V3.2 introduced DeepSeek Sparse Attention, where a light indexer picks which earlier tokens matter. As of late 2026, DeepSeek-V4 combines compressed and sparse attention and reports needing 10% of the KV cache and 27% of the per-token compute of V3.2 at a one-million-token context.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Replacing attention: linear and hybrid models\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Linear attention\u003c/b> and \u003cb>state-space\u003c/b> layers (Mamba, Gated DeltaNet) work like a learnt RNN: each layer keeps a fixed-size state and updates it with every token, so memory and per-token compute do not grow with context. They are weaker at exact copying and lookup from far back, which full attention does well. The 2025 to 2026 answer is \u003cb>hybrids\u003c/b>: Qwen3-Next and Qwen3.8 use three Gated DeltaNet layers for every full-attention layer, so only a quarter of the layers hold a growing KV cache.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Before choosing an open model for long context or many concurrent users, compute KV bytes per token from config.json. It is a property of the architecture, not the parameter count: Qwen3-0.6B stores 112 KiB per token, almost as much as Llama 3.1 8B.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check that your serving engine supports the variant efficiently. vLLM and SGLang handle GQA, MLA, sliding windows and the main hybrids, but confirm it for the exact model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every saving carries a quality risk: fewer KV heads, compression, windows and linear layers each trade some recall. These choices are fixed at pretraining, so they arrive with the model you pick.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Llama 3.1 8B has 32 query heads and 8 key-value heads. How many query heads share each KV head, and how much smaller is its cache than an MHA version?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>4 query heads share each KV head, so the cache is 4 times smaller: 128 KiB per token instead of 512 KiB.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a sliding-window layer's cache stop growing, and what does the model give up?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The layer only attends to the last W tokens, so older keys and values can be discarded and the cache is capped at W tokens. It gives up direct access to distant tokens; precise recall of far-back details then depends on full-attention layers or on information relayed upward through the stack.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does MLA store instead of per-head keys and values?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One compressed latent vector per token per layer (512 numbers in DeepSeek-V3) plus a small shared rotary key (64 numbers); per-head keys and values are reconstructed from the latent at attention time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do hybrid models keep some full-attention layers?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Linear-attention and state-space layers squeeze the whole history into a fixed-size state, which loses exact details. A few full-attention layers keep precise lookup and copying while most layers stay cheap.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:mixture-of-experts-models":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In a \u003cb>mixture-of-experts (MoE)\u003c/b> model, the MLP in each block is replaced by many smaller MLPs called \u003cb>experts\u003c/b> plus a \u003cb>router\u003c/b> that sends each token to only a few of them. The model has a huge total parameter count, which gives it capacity to store knowledge, but each token uses only a small active subset, which sets the compute per token. You pay memory for total parameters and compute for active ones.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How routing works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each token at each MoE layer:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>The router, a small linear layer, gives every expert a score.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The top k experts are chosen (k is between 2 and 10 in current models).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Their scores become gate weights through a softmax over the chosen experts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The output is the gate-weighted sum of the chosen experts' outputs. Many models add one or more \u003cb>shared experts\u003c/b> that every token always uses, for common knowledge.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>A worked example with 8 experts and top-2 routing:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>router scores:  e0 2.0   e1 0.5   e2 1.2   e3 -1.0   e4 0.1   e5 3.0   e6 0.0   e7 -0.5\ntop 2:          e5 (3.0) and e0 (2.0)\ngates:          softmax([3.0, 2.0]) = [0.731, 0.269]\noutput:         0.731 × expert5(x) + 0.269 × expert0(x)      (the other six experts do no work)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Routing happens per token and per layer, so neighbouring tokens use different experts. Experts are not tidy topic specialists: the Mixtral paper found no obvious assignment by topic (arXiv papers, biology and philosophy used experts in similar proportions), while routing did follow syntax, for example indentation tokens in code going to the same experts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Total versus active in real models\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>Experts per MoE layer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Used per token\u003c/th>\u003cth>Total parameters\u003c/th>\u003cth>Active parameters\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mixtral 8x7B (2023)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>47B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>13B\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DeepSeek-V3 (2024)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>256 routed + 1 shared\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8 + 1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>671B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>37B\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>gpt-oss-120b (2025)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>117B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5.1B\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Qwen3-30B-A3B (2025)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3B\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Qwen3.8 (2026)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>512 routed + 1 shared\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10 + 1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.4T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>95B\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The trend is towards many small experts and very low activity: about 3 to 5% of parameters per token in the newest large models. Names often say it: \"A3B\" means about 3 billion active.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Memory versus compute\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Memory follows total parameters.\u003c/b> Every expert must sit in GPU memory because any token might need it. DeepSeek-V3's 671B parameters take about 671 GB at 8 bits each, a multi-GPU server. gpt-oss-120b ships its experts in a 4-bit format (MXFP4) so the whole model fits on one 80 GB GPU.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compute follows active parameters.\u003c/b> A forward pass costs about 2 FLOPs (floating-point operations) per active parameter per token, so DeepSeek-V3 does roughly the work of a 37B dense model: about 74 billion FLOPs per token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Speed is subtler.\u003c/b> Decoding is limited by memory reads (Inference module). For a single user only the chosen experts' weights are read per token, so an MoE decodes about as fast as a dense model of its active size. In a large batch, different tokens pick different experts, nearly all experts are read every step and the advantage shrinks; MoE serving relies on big batches and on spreading experts across GPUs (expert parallelism, Serving module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Training wrinkles\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Routers tend to collapse onto a few favourite experts, leaving others untrained. Fixes include an auxiliary \u003cb>load-balancing loss\u003c/b>, DeepSeek's bias-adjustment method that avoids the extra loss, and a \u003cb>capacity factor\u003c/b> that caps how many tokens an expert takes per batch (overflow tokens skip the layer). Fine-tuning MoEs is also more delicate than fine-tuning dense models.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For API users, MoE explains why very large models can be cheap and fast per token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For self-hosters, size hardware by total parameters plus the KV cache (which depends on the attention design, not on experts), and expect throughput to depend on batch size more than with dense models.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When comparing models, use active parameters for speed and cost and total parameters for memory and, loosely, knowledge capacity.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain why MoE models have more parameters than they use per token.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each MoE layer holds many expert MLPs, but a router sends each token to only the top few (plus any shared experts). All experts count towards the total and must be stored, yet a token passes through only the selected ones, so the active parameters, and the compute per token, are a small fraction of the total: 37B of 671B in DeepSeek-V3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">gpt-oss-120b has 117B total and 5.1B active parameters, with expert weights at about 4.25 bits each. Roughly how much memory do the weights need, and why does it decode quickly for one user?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 117 billion × 4.25 ÷ 8 bytes, roughly 62 GB (a little more in practice, since attention and embedding weights stay at higher precision), which fits on one 80 GB GPU. Each token reads and computes with only about 5B parameters, so single-user decoding is close to the speed of a 5B dense model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the MoE speed advantage shrink at large batch sizes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each token in the batch picks its own experts, so with many tokens almost every expert is chosen by someone and all expert weights must be read every step, as in a dense model of the total size.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a shared expert?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An expert that every token uses in addition to its routed experts, meant to hold common knowledge so the routed experts can specialise. DeepSeek-V3 has one shared and 256 routed experts per MoE layer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:hallucination-and-why-it-happens":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A model always produces a plausible continuation, whether or not it knows the answer. Hallucinations come from facts it saw rarely or never, from training and evaluations that reward a confident guess over \"I don't know\", and from decoding that commits to one token at a time. You cannot remove them from the model, but you can cut them sharply by putting the facts in the context, permitting and rewarding abstention, and verifying outputs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What counts as a hallucination\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>hallucination\u003c/b> is fluent output that is false or unsupported. Two kinds matter in products:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unfaithful to the provided context\u003c/b>: the summary says the contract ends in May when the document says March.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unsupported by the world\u003c/b>: a made-up citation, an invented API parameter, a wrong birth date, a fabricated quote.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Why it happens\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>The objective rewards plausibility.\u003c/b> Pretraining maximises the probability of likely text. A false sentence with the right shape scores well; fluency is not truth.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rare facts are hard to learn.\u003c/b> Kalai and colleagues (OpenAI, 2025) argue that after pretraining, the error rate on arbitrary facts should be at least the fraction of those facts that appeared exactly once in the training data. If 20% of people's birthdays appear only once, expect a base model to be wrong on at least 20% of birthday questions. Frequently repeated facts, such as Einstein's birthday, are rarely wrong.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Guessing is rewarded.\u003c/b> Most benchmarks score answers as simply right or wrong, so \"I don't know\" earns 0 while a guess earns something on average. Post-training that optimises for those scores teaches models to guess:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>The model is 30% sure of an answer.\nRight/wrong grading:          guess = 0.3 × 1 + 0.7 × 0    = +0.3    abstain = 0   -&gt; guessing wins\nWrong answers cost a point:   guess = 0.3 × 1 + 0.7 × (-1) = -0.4    abstain = 0   -&gt; abstaining wins\n(with +1 / -1 / 0 scoring, guessing only pays when the model is more than 50% sure)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Snowballing.\u003c/b> Generation commits one token at a time and never revises. Once a wrong name or number is emitted, later tokens condition on it and build a consistent but false story.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Missing knowledge.\u003c/b> Events after the training cutoff and your private data were never seen, so the model fills the gap with something plausible.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Context problems.\u003c/b> The right passage is buried in a long prompt, two sources conflict, or the question contains a false premise that the model goes along with (\u003cb>sycophancy\u003c/b>).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Calibration\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>calibrated\u003c/b> model's 70%-confident answers are right about 70% of the time. Pretrained models are often fairly well calibrated about their token probabilities, and OpenAI's GPT-4 technical report showed post-training made calibration worse. Reasoning models check their work and do better on maths and code, but they still hallucinate facts and can invent results when a tool call fails.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mitigations matched to causes\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Cause\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mitigation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Covered in\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fact missing, rare or recent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieval (RAG), web search, database and API tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieval stage, Tools\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Guessing rewarded\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Explicitly permit \"I don't know\"; evals that penalise confident errors more than abstentions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompting, Evals\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Unsupported claims\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Require citations to supplied sources and check that each quote exists\u003c/td>\u003ctd>RAG\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exact arithmetic and lookups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code execution, calculators, structured queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tools\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Snowballing in long outputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Split tasks into verified steps; ask for evidence before conclusions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>False premises and sycophancy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Instruct the model to check premises; phrase questions neutrally\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Residual errors\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Groundedness checks by an LLM judge; consistency across several samples; human review for high stakes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Evals, Product\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Measure hallucination on your own eval set (claims checked against sources), not by impression.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Design the product so errors are visible and cheap: show sources, let users check, keep a human in the loop where mistakes are costly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Lower temperature reduces random slips but does not supply missing knowledge; a model can be confidently wrong at temperature 0.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you provoke a hallucination and then fix it with grounding?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Ask about something plausible but obscure or private, such as a specific clause of an internal policy, the parameters of a little-known library function, or the birth year of a minor public figure; the model will often answer fluently and wrongly. Then paste the authoritative source (the policy text, the library docs) into the prompt, instruct the model to answer only from it, to quote the supporting passage and to say \"not in the document\" if the answer is absent. Check the quote against the source: the answer should now be correct or an explicit abstention.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does right-or-wrong grading encourage hallucination?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An abstention scores 0 while a guess has a positive expected score, so a model optimised for such scores learns to guess confidently. Penalising wrong answers more than abstentions removes the incentive.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is snowballing, and how do you counter it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Generation commits token by token, so an early error becomes context that later tokens stay consistent with, producing a coherent but false answer. Counter it by splitting the task into verified steps, asking for evidence before the conclusion, or checking key claims with tools.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A coding assistant keeps inventing parameters for your internal SDK. Which mitigations fit?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Put the SDK's real documentation or type signatures in the context through retrieval or a docs tool, and verify output by type-checking, compiling or running tests so invented parameters fail fast.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Try it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ask a model for three academic papers, with authors, year and venue, on a narrow topic you know well, and check each one. Then ask again, adding \"If you are not certain a paper exists, say so\", and allow web search if the tool is available. Compare the two answers.\u003c/p>","c:llm-visualisation":"\u003cp>In part A you measure what tokenisers do to your own kinds of text. In part B you watch a complete GPT run, layer by layer, so the diagrams from this module become concrete.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A browser\u003c/li>\u003cli>Python 3.11 or later with \u003ccode>pip install tiktoken tokenizers\u003c/code> (the \u003ccode>tokenizers\u003c/code> library downloads only tokeniser files, a few megabytes each)\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Part A: tokenisers (15 minutes)\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>In Tiktokenizer, paste a paragraph you wrote recently. Note the token count under \u003ccode>cl100k_base\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>o200k_base\u003c/code>, and the ratio of characters to tokens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Paste the same paragraph in another language (use a translator if needed), a 15-line function from your own code, a JSON object from an API you use and a sentence containing a 12-digit number. Record each count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare three model families locally:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import tiktoken\nfrom tokenizers import Tokenizer\n\ntexts = {\n    \"english\": \"The meeting is moved to Thursday at 3pm because the client asked for more time.\",\n    \"code\": \"def mean(xs):\\n    return sum(xs) / len(xs) if xs else 0.0\\n\",\n    \"json\": '{\"order_id\": 18734, \"status\": \"shipped\", \"items\": [{\"sku\": \"A-17\", \"qty\": 2}]}',\n    \"number\": \"Invoice total: 1234567.89 EUR\",\n}\nopenai = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"o200k_base\")\nqwen = Tokenizer.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\")\ngpt_oss = Tokenizer.from_pretrained(\"openai/gpt-oss-20b\")\n\nfor label, text in texts.items():\n    print(label, len(openai.encode(text)), len(qwen.encode(text).ids), len(gpt_oss.encode(text).ids))\nprint(qwen.encode(texts[\"number\"]).tokens)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Add your own texts from step 2 to the dictionary and rerun.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Look for three surprises: a common word split into odd pieces, a word whose split changes with a leading space or capital letter, and how each tokeniser handles digits.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Part B: a GPT you can see (20 minutes)\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Open the LLM visualisation. The walkthrough uses \u003cb>nano-gpt\u003c/b>, a GPT of about 85,000 parameters trained to sort a sequence of six letters (A, B and C) into alphabetical order. Its vocabulary is just three tokens, with 3 layers, 3 heads and an embedding width of 48.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Step through the embedding stage. Note that each input position gets a token embedding plus a position embedding (this older GPT design learns positions instead of using RoPE), giving one 48-number column per token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Step through the first block. Find the layer norm, the query, key and value vectors for each head, the attention matrix, and confirm that its upper triangle is empty: the causal mask. Watch the attention output added back into the residual stream.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Step through the MLP: each token's vector is expanded to four times its width (48 to 192), passed through a GELU non-linearity and projected back to 48, then added to the stream.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Skip to the output: the final layer norm, the LM head producing three logits and the softmax. Check that the most probable token is the correct next letter of the sorted sequence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Switch the view to GPT-2 small (12 layers, 12 heads, width 768) and then to the largest model shown. The structure is identical; only the sizes change.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>English is the cheapest text in every tokeniser, at around 4 to 5 characters per token; your other language, code and JSON cost noticeably more.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tokenisers disagree. In the script above, o200k_base and gpt-oss give the same counts (gpt-oss uses an o200k-based vocabulary), while Qwen3 splits every digit into its own token: the number sentence is 10 tokens for o200k_base and 15 for Qwen3.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In nano-gpt the attention matrices are lower-triangular, the residual stream keeps the same width through every block, and the output is a probability for each of the three tokens.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do o200k_base and Qwen3 give different counts for the same number, and why might a model designer choose one-digit tokens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Their vocabularies were built with different pre-tokenisation rules: o200k_base groups up to three digits into one token, while Qwen3 splits numbers into single digits. Single-digit tokens make place value consistent (every digit is its own token), which tends to help arithmetic, at the cost of more tokens for long numbers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In nano-gpt, where would you look to confirm the model cannot see future tokens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In the attention matrix of any head: entries above the diagonal (a query attending to a later key) are empty or zero because the causal mask set them to minus infinity before the softmax.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:llms/gpt-from-scratch-small":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a GPT in PyTorch from an empty file, train it on a small text corpus and make it generate text in that style. This is a standalone build, not part of Atlas. The goal is understanding: by the end you should be able to explain, line by line, the tokeniser, embeddings, causal self-attention, multi-head attention, the MLP, residual connections, layer norm, the training loss and sampling. Following Karpathy's two-hour video \"Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out\" (linked under Go deeper) while typing every line yourself is the recommended path.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A text corpus of 1 to 5 MB: the classic tiny Shakespeare file used in the video, or something you care about (your own notes, a public-domain author, a codebase)\u003c/li>\u003cli>A character-level tokeniser you write yourself (\u003ccode>stoi\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>itos\u003c/code> dictionaries), with a 90/10 train and validation split\u003c/li>\u003cli>A bigram baseline model first, then the transformer\u003c/li>\u003cli>Causal multi-head self-attention, an MLP, residual connections and layer norm, written by you (no \u003ccode>nn.Transformer\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>nn.MultiheadAttention\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\u003cli>A training loop that reports training and validation loss at intervals\u003c/li>\u003cli>A \u003ccode>generate()\u003c/code> function with temperature\u003c/li>\u003cli>A written explanation (\u003ccode>NOTES.md\u003c/code>) of every component and of your experiments\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data and tokeniser.\u003c/b> Load the text, build the character vocabulary, write \u003ccode>encode\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>decode\u003c/code>, and a \u003ccode>get_batch\u003c/code> function returning random input and target blocks where targets are inputs shifted by one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bigram baseline.\u003c/b> A model that is only an embedding table of vocabulary by vocabulary logits. Train it and sample. Record its validation loss; it is your floor to beat.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One head of self-attention.\u003c/b> Add token and position embeddings, a single causal attention head (queries, keys, values, scaling, a lower-triangular mask) and the LM head. Confirm the loss drops below the bigram's.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Full block.\u003c/b> Multi-head attention with an output projection, a 4x MLP, residual adds and pre-norm layer norm. Stack several blocks; add dropout.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Train properly.\u003c/b> Use AdamW, evaluate on validation every few hundred steps and save the best checkpoint. On a GPU, the video's settings (6 layers, 6 heads, width 384, context 256, batch 64, learning rate 3e-4, dropout 0.2) reach a validation loss of about 1.5 on tiny Shakespeare; on a laptop CPU use 4 layers, 4 heads, width 128, context 64 and expect about 1.9.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generate.\u003c/b> Sample 500 characters at temperatures 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 and describe the differences.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Experiments.\u003c/b> Change one thing at a time and record validation loss: number of layers, context length, removing the residual connections, removing the position embeddings. Explain each result.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Write-up.\u003c/b> In \u003ccode>NOTES.md\u003c/code>, explain each component in two to four sentences, including tensor shapes (B, T, C), and include your experiment table and three samples.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python train.py\u003c/code> trains from scratch and prints falling training and validation losses, and \u003ccode>python sample.py\u003c/code> generates text recognisably in the style of your corpus\u003c/li>\u003cli>Your transformer clearly beats your bigram baseline on validation loss\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>NOTES.md\u003c/code> explains every component and every experiment in your own words, with the numbers\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Replace the character tokeniser with a small BPE tokeniser you train yourself (Karpathy's tokeniser video is the guide)\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a KV cache to \u003ccode>generate()\u003c/code> and measure the speed-up for 1,000 generated characters (the Inference module explains why it helps)\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace learnt position embeddings with RoPE, and attention with grouped-query attention (2 KV heads), and compare loss and speed\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read nanochat to see how the same ideas are arranged in a full modern training and chat pipeline\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Print tensor shapes everywhere at first. Most bugs are a transposed matrix or a mask with the wrong shape.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The mask must be applied before the softmax, with \u003ccode>-inf\u003c/code>, not after it with zeros.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the loss starts much above ln(vocabulary size), about 4.2 for 65 characters, your initialisation or loss call is wrong; if it stops falling early, lower the learning rate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>torch.no_grad()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>model.eval()\u003c/code> for evaluation and generation, and crop the context to the block size inside \u003ccode>generate()\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
